[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Routing in Node.js: Defining Routes and Handling HTTP Methods"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Routing is a crucial aspect of building web applications with Node.js. It involves defining how your application responds to client requests to specific endpoints (URIs) and HTTP request methods (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE, etc.). This tutorial will guide you through defining routes and handling different HTTP methods using Express.js, a popular Node.js web application framework."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Setting up an Express.js Application"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"First, ensure you have Node.js and npm (Node Package Manager) installed. Create a new project directory and initialize a Node.js project:"},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"mkdir my-node-app
cd my-node-app
npm init -y"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"Next, install Express.js:"},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"npm install express"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Defining Basic Routes"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Create a file named 'app.js' (or any name you prefer) and add the following code to define basic routes:"},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"const express = require('express');
const app = express();
const port = 3000;

app.get('/', (req, res) => {
  res.send('Hello World!');
});

app.listen(port, () => {
  console.log(`Example app listening at http://localhost:${port}`);
});"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"This code creates an Express application, defines a route for the root path ('/') that responds with 'Hello World!', and starts the server on port 3000.  To run the application, execute:"},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"node app.js"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Open your browser and navigate to 'http://localhost:3000' to see the 'Hello World!' message."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Handling Different HTTP Methods"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Express.js provides methods for handling different HTTP request methods. Here's how to handle GET, POST, PUT, and DELETE requests:"},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"app.get('/users', (req, res) => {
  res.send('GET request to the /users route');
});

app.post('/users', (req, res) => {
  res.send('POST request to the /users route');
});

app.put('/users/:userId', (req, res) => {
  const userId = req.params.userId;
  res.send(`PUT request to update user with ID: ${userId}`);
});

app.delete('/users/:userId', (req, res) => {
  const userId = req.params.userId;
  res.send(`DELETE request to delete user with ID: ${userId}`);
});"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example:"},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"app.get() handles GET requests to '/users'."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"app.post() handles POST requests to '/users'."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"app.put() handles PUT requests to '/users/:userId', where ':userId' is a route parameter.  The value of 'userId' can be accessed using 'req.params.userId'."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"app.delete() handles DELETE requests to '/users/:userId', similar to the PUT request."},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"Using Middleware"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"Middleware functions are functions that have access to the request object (req), the response object (res), and the next middleware function in the application’s request-response cycle. Middleware functions can perform tasks such as:"},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Executing any code."},{"order":25,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Making changes to the request and the response objects."},{"order":26,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Ending the request-response cycle."},{"order":27,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Calling the next middleware function in the stack."},{"order":28,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here's an example of using middleware to log request information:"},{"order":29,"type":"code","text":"app.use((req, res, next) => {
  console.log(`Request received: ${req.method} ${req.url}`);
  next(); // Call the next middleware function
});"},{"order":30,"type":"paragraph","text":"This middleware function logs the request method and URL for every incoming request. The 'next()' function is crucial; it passes control to the next middleware function in the stack. Without it, the request will be left hanging."},{"order":31,"type":"subheading","text":"Route Parameters"},{"order":32,"type":"paragraph","text":"Route parameters are named URL segments that are used to capture the values specified at their position in the URL. These captured values are populated in 'req.params'. We saw an example of this with the '/users/:userId' route. Here's another example:"},{"order":33,"type":"code","text":"app.get('/products/:productId/reviews/:reviewId', (req, res) => {
  const productId = req.params.productId;
  const reviewId = req.params.reviewId;
  res.send(`Product ID: ${productId}, Review ID: ${reviewId}`);
});"},{"order":34,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this case, accessing '/products/123/reviews/456' would result in 'productId' being '123' and 'reviewId' being '456'."},{"order":35,"type":"subheading","text":"Query Parameters"},{"order":36,"type":"paragraph","text":"Query parameters are key-value pairs that appear after a question mark (?) in the URL. They are accessed using 'req.query'."},{"order":37,"type":"code","text":"app.get('/search', (req, res) => {
  const searchTerm = req.query.q;
  res.send(`Search term: ${searchTerm}`);
});"},{"order":38,"type":"paragraph","text":"Accessing '/search?q=example' would result in 'searchTerm' being 'example'."},{"order":39,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":40,"type":"paragraph","text":"This tutorial covered the basics of routing in Node.js using Express.js, including defining routes, handling different HTTP methods, using middleware, and accessing route and query parameters. Understanding these concepts is essential for building robust and scalable web applications."}]
